  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 915 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Heat and humidity persists through midweek with\nheat indices in the upper 90s to near 105F.\n\nLatest analysis shows the offshore subtropical ridge continuing to \nprevail, as a trough from the Great Lakes is moving into the \nNortheast CONUS, leading to zonal flow for the area today and \ntomorrow. At the surface, the Bermuda high remains in place helping \nthe hot and humid conditions before breaking down over the next few \ndays. \n\nThe hot and humid conditions will continue over the next couple of \ndays. Dewpoints look to mix out some Mon, with heat indices likely \nreaching the upper 90s to lower 100s. The best potential for any \nhead headlines is Tues, as dew points look to be in the lower to mid \n70s with heat indices in the upper 90s to lower 100s and values \naround 105F in SE VA/NE NC where the cloud cover looks to be less. \nModel ensembles depict a cold front crossing the area late week, \nwhich could help lower temperatures and dew points Fri into the \nweekend. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Low chance of showers/storms today and Monday \nwith a more unsettled pattern returning Tuesday through \nThursday. Storms Tuesday and Wednesday may bring damaging wind \ngusts and localized flooding.\n\nWith the aforementioned zonal flow across the area through tomorrow,\nshower/storm chances will be low across the area. A few stray\nshowers or storms will be possible through midnight, mainly west\nof I-95. Additional isolated showers possible north of RIC\ntomorrow afternoon and evening and in NE NC tomorrow evening.\n\nA more unsettled pattern evolves Tues through Thurs. The zonal flow \npattern is expected to break down, as a ridge aloft starts to build \nin Tuesday across the central United States. This will allow for WNW \nflow aloft to return to the area. While at the surface, the Bermuda \nhigh will start to break down slightly and move over the the Gulf \nstates. This could allow for multiple days of active weather (Tues-\nThurs) to return to the area. However, there still remains quite a \nbit of uncertainty at this time, due to some slight disagreement in \nthe models in the lowest levels of the atmosphere. Confidence is \nhighest of shower/storm development on Tues with 30-60% PoPs across \nthe area, highest north. Storm development Wed and Thurs will depend \non leftover boundaries and meso features. SPC has the majority of \nthe area in a Marginal Risk (level 1/5) of severe weather Tuesday \nand a Day 4 15% chance of severe weather, which translates to a \nSlight Risk (level 2/5) for a large portion of the area Wed. \nDamaging winds and localized flooding are the primary concerns with \nany storm.",
